{"id": "2026-07-28-news-m7-1-quake-strikes-southern-japan", "date": "2026-07-28", "stream": "news", "headline": "M7.1 quake strikes southern Japan", "summary": "A magnitude-7.1 earthquake hit Kumamoto on Kyushu on 28 July, injuring at least 50 and prompting a tsunami advisory.", "url": "https://abcnews.com/International/magnitude-71-earthquake-rocks-japan-tsunami-warning-issued/story?id=135143882", "source_domain": "abcnews.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["new source"], "topics": ["world", "science"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "A magnitude-7.1 earthquake struck southwestern Japan on Tuesday 28 July, hitting Kumamoto prefecture on the island of Kyushu and injuring at least 50 people. The quake struck shortly before 16:30 local time at a shallow depth of about 10 km (roughly 6 miles), according to the US Geological Survey; the Japan Meteorological Agency (JMA) issued a tsunami advisory for waves of about one metre along the Ariake and Yatsushiro seas and urged people away from the coast. Several thousand homes lost power, rail services stopped, and Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi warned of strong shaking around Uki City and Makigawa Town. Japan's Nuclear Regulation Authority reported no abnormalities at nearby nuclear plants. Kumamoto is the same prefecture devastated by a pair of major quakes in 2016.", "why": "Japan sits on the \"Ring of Fire\" and records roughly a fifth of the world's earthquakes, but a shallow M7.1 directly under a populated region is at the severe end and the tsunami and landslide risk will keep the response tense for days.", "thread_id": "2026-07-28-news-m7-1-quake-strikes-southern-japan", "first_seen_date": "2026-07-28", "event_date": "2026-07-28", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Japan", "Kumamoto", "Kyushu", "Japan Meteorological Agency", "US Geological Survey", "Sanae Takaichi"]}
{"id": "2026-07-28-news-netanyahu-and-zelenskyy-meet-trump", "date": "2026-07-28", "stream": "news", "headline": "Netanyahu and Zelenskyy meet Trump", "summary": "Netanyahu and Zelenskyy both met Trump in Washington on 28 July as a Senate Russia-sanctions vote loomed and the Iran and Ukraine wars converged.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/7/28/ukraines-zelenskyy-set-to-meet-trump-as-iran-and-ukraine-wars-converge", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy both descended on Washington on Tuesday 28 July to meet President Donald Trump, as a long-stalled Russia-sanctions bill neared a Senate vote and the wars in Iran and Ukraine visibly converged. Both leaders were in the US chiefly for the funeral of Senator Lindsey Graham, a Trump ally and hawkish backer of both Israel and Ukraine who died on 11 July; Netanyahu was set to meet Trump with the US-Israel war on Iran topping the agenda, while Zelenskyy pressed for anti-ballistic-missile defences and help producing Patriot systems. The sanctions bill Graham championed would impose 100% tariffs on the five largest buyers of Russian oil and gas - including China and India - and Trump has moved at the last minute to fold sanctions on Iran into the same package. The convergence is concrete: Ukraine attacked an Iranian tanker in the Caspian Sea on Saturday 25 July that it says was ferrying drones and components to Russia, Iran has vowed to retaliate, and Russia said more than 390 Ukrainian drones struck the Moscow region overnight. Trump has said talks with Iran are under way during the current pause in US strikes.", "why": "Two wars 2,500 km apart are being wired together through Washington's sanctions machinery and Iran's arms pipeline to Moscow - treat both leaders' framing as advocacy, since each is seeking Trump's backing.", "thread_id": "2026-07-28-news-netanyahu-and-zelenskyy-meet-trump", "first_seen_date": "2026-07-28", "event_date": "2026-07-28", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Benjamin Netanyahu", "Volodymyr Zelenskyy", "Donald Trump", "Lindsey Graham", "Iran", "Russia", "Ukraine", "United States"]}
{"id": "2026-07-28-news-seoul-s-kospi-plunges-10-8", "date": "2026-07-28", "stream": "news", "headline": "Seoul's Kospi plunges 10.8%", "summary": "South Korea's Kospi fell 10.8% on 28 July as Samsung and SK Hynix crashed on fears over China's chipmaking advances.", "url": "https://www.ksat.com/business/2026/07/28/south-koreas-kospi-index-sinks-more-than-10-on-heavy-selling-of-chipmaking-stocks/", "source_domain": "ksat.com", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["new source"], "topics": ["economy", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "South Korea's Kospi stock index collapsed 10.8% on Tuesday 28 July, closing at 6,023.66 - its lowest since April - after a rout in chipmaking shares triggered trading circuit-breakers (automatic pauses meant to halt panic selling). Samsung Electronics sank 13.4% and SK Hynix 14.7%; together the two account for a large share of the Seoul market. The sell-off followed a report in the trade publication The Information that China has begun mass-producing homegrown deep-ultraviolet (DUV) lithography tools - the machines that print circuit patterns onto silicon wafers, and a capability Western and Korean firms had assumed China was years from matching - stoking fears that Chinese competition could erode the incumbents' lead. European markets opened slightly higher, suggesting the shock stayed largely regional for now.", "why": "Semiconductors are the backbone of South Korea's export economy, so a one-day drop this steep is a real macroeconomic event - and it signals investors are newly worried that China's chip progress could reset a market the West has dominated.", "thread_id": "2026-07-28-news-seoul-s-kospi-plunges-10-8", "first_seen_date": "2026-07-28", "event_date": "2026-07-28", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["South Korea", "Kospi", "Samsung Electronics", "SK Hynix", "China", "The Information"]}
{"id": "2026-07-28-news-sudan-s-rsf-chief-vows-to-unleash-fighters", "date": "2026-07-28", "stream": "news", "headline": "Sudan's RSF chief vows to unleash fighters", "summary": "RSF leader Hemedti said on 27 July he will no longer restrain his fighters after the army retook the strategic El-Obeid-Khartoum road.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/articles/le-chef-des-paramilitaires-au-soudan-ne-retiendra-plus-ses-troupes", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["geopolitics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The head of Sudan's paramilitary Rapid Support Forces (RSF), Mohamed Hamdan Daglo (\"Hemedti\"), said on Monday 27 July he would \"no longer hold back\" his fighters, after his forces suffered a battlefield reverse near El-Obeid. Sudan's army said it had pushed the RSF westward and regained control of the strategic 400-km road linking El-Obeid, in North Kordofan, to the capital Khartoum, where the paramilitaries had massed for months in preparation for a large-scale assault; army chief Abdel Fattah al-Burhan toured a recaptured position alongside allied militia commanders. The RSF, whose forces stand accused of genocide in Sudan's two-year civil war, has been fighting the regular army since April 2023.", "why": "Hemedti's threat to unleash his fighters after losing ground points to a bloodier phase in a war that has already driven the world's largest displacement crisis, and the road's recapture eases pressure on the army's supply line to Khartoum.", "thread_id": "2026-07-28-news-sudan-s-rsf-chief-vows-to-unleash-fighters", "first_seen_date": "2026-07-28", "event_date": "2026-07-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Sudan", "Rapid Support Forces", "Mohamed Hamdan Daglo", "Abdel Fattah al-Burhan", "El-Obeid", "Khartoum"]}
{"id": "2026-07-28-news-chad-moves-to-quit-the-icc", "date": "2026-07-28", "stream": "news", "headline": "Chad moves to quit the ICC", "summary": "Chad announced it will withdraw from the International Criminal Court, calling its record selective, and urged other African states to follow.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/chad-s-government-says-it-s-withdrawing-from-the-icc/a-78132322", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["geopolitics", "world"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Chad announced it will withdraw from the International Criminal Court (ICC), the Hague-based tribunal that prosecutes war crimes and crimes against humanity, and called on other African Union members to follow. Chad's foreign ministry said the court's record shows \"undeniable and egregious selectivity,\" noting that nine of the ICC's 13 investigations since 2002 have concerned African states and that only one of seven people detained came from outside Africa; the ministry framed the exit as confidence in national and African justice, not an abandonment of victims. That selectivity charge is Chad's own characterisation - the ICC counters that several of its African cases were opened at the request of the states themselves - and it lands as Chad's military leader faces opposition accusations of repression.", "why": "An African state quitting the ICC and lobbying the AU to do the same feeds a long-running push to weaken the court's reach on the continent, at a moment when its authority is already contested.", "thread_id": "2026-07-28-news-chad-moves-to-quit-the-icc", "first_seen_date": "2026-07-28", "event_date": "2026-07-28", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Chad", "International Criminal Court", "African Union"]}
{"id": "2026-07-28-news-us-court-rejects-credit-suisse-at1-suit", "date": "2026-07-28", "stream": "news", "headline": "US court rejects Credit Suisse AT1 suit", "summary": "A US appeals court upheld dismissal of an investor suit against Switzerland over the CS AT1 bond write-down, citing state immunity.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/credit-suisse-notuebernahme-us-berufungsgericht-lehnt-at1-klage-gegen-die-schweiz-ab", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "A US appeals court has rejected an investor lawsuit against Switzerland over the wipeout of Credit Suisse's AT1 bonds, the federal finance department (EFD) confirmed on Tuesday 28 July. The judges upheld a lower court's finding that when Switzerland's regulator wrote the bonds to zero it acted as a supervisory authority, not a market participant, and so enjoys state immunity and falls outside US jurisdiction. AT1 bonds (\"additional tier 1\" - subordinated debt created after the 2008 crisis that is designed to absorb losses and prop up a failing bank) worth about US$17.3 billion were written off when the Swiss state orchestrated UBS's emergency takeover of Credit Suisse in spring 2023. A group of international investors had sued in June 2024 for roughly US$370 million; they can still seek review by the full appeals court or the US Supreme Court, though the EFD says the bar for that is \"very high.\" Separate cases continue elsewhere: Switzerland's own Federal Administrative Court partly overturned the write-down in October 2025 after complaints from some 3,600 investors - a ruling FINMA and UBS have appealed to the Federal Supreme Court - and a group of Japanese investors filed an arbitration claim at the ICSID (a World Bank body that settles investment disputes) in late 2025.", "why": "The US ruling closes one front in the global legal fight over the most contested piece of the CS rescue, but the decisive battles over whether the write-down stands are still open in Swiss courts and international arbitration.", "thread_id": "2026-07-28-news-us-court-rejects-credit-suisse-at1-suit", "first_seen_date": "2026-07-28", "event_date": "2026-07-28", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "Credit Suisse", "UBS", "FINMA", "EFD", "ICSID"]}
{"id": "2026-07-28-news-new-heatwave-grips-parched-switzerland", "date": "2026-07-28", "stream": "news", "headline": "New heatwave grips parched Switzerland", "summary": "A fresh heatwave with peaks of 34-39C is forecast for Switzerland from midweek as drought keeps fire and fireworks bans in place before 1 August.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/temperaturen-ueber-30-grad-die-naechste-hitzewelle-rollt-an-und-abkuehlung-in-weiter-ferne", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "science"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "A fresh heatwave is rolling into an already parched Switzerland, with SRF Meteo forecasting temperatures of 34-39C at their peak on Thursday 30 July and the hot spell likely to last into the first week of August. The 30C mark was expected to be passed again in the north on Tuesday, widespread 35C from Wednesday 29 July, with the highest readings in northwest Switzerland, the lower Aare valley and around Geneva, plus several \"tropical nights\" (when temperatures stay above 20C) in cities and by the larger lakes. Forecasters see no substantial cooling or the sustained, widespread rain needed to break the drought before mid-August; open-fire and fireworks bans remain in force across much of the country ahead of the 1 August national holiday (this Saturday), and a field fire near Koblenz (Aargau) spread into woodland on Tuesday.", "why": "This is the same heat-and-drought spell fuelling the wildfires in France and Spain now reaching Swiss soil, and it means the country's biggest civic holiday will again be marked under near-total fire restrictions.", "thread_id": "2026-07-13-news-switzerland-braces-for-new-heatwave-as-2026-near", "first_seen_date": "2026-07-13", "event_date": "2026-07-28", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "SRF Meteo", "Aargau", "Koblenz", "Geneva"]}
